WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.009
Jaws is widely considered one of the best
thrillers ever made, but it almost never happened

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:05.509
at all.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.400
From budget problems to cast feuds and Oscar
snubs, we're diving deep into the waters of

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.020
a movie that started with notorious production
problems but finished with rave reviews, three

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.520
Academy Awards, and a whole bunch of cash.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.680
Here's the untold truth of Jaws.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.360
Unfinished script

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.840
One of the early signs that production on
Jaws wouldn't run as smoothly as planned came

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.300
from the script, which passed through the
hands of three writers — Jaws novelist Peter

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.320
Benchley, playwright Howard Sackler, and comedy
writer Carl Gottlieb.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:39.820
Gottlieb told the Los Angeles Times in 2015:
"[Benchley was a] journalist who didn't understand

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:40.440
screenplays.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.700
Sackler did a workman-like job of translating
the novel faithfully.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.000
A faithful rendition of the novel was not
what was needed.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.120
Something else was needed, and I came on board."

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.820
Gottlieb wound up removing a number of subplots
from Benchley's novel and adding a dose of

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.700
humor to the script, ultimately creating the
story that made it on-screen.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.280
Broken shark

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.630
Choosing to film out in the ocean and not
in a Hollywood studio tank created a number

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.560
of headaches for the cast and crew.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.440
However, few things were more frustrating
— and costly — than the film's mechanical

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:09.940
shark.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.080
According to Mental Floss, the shark sank
to the bottom of the Nantucket Sound on its

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.400
first day of shooting.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.120
"And you could hear, basically and every day
you could hear this 'the shark is not working.'

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.259
The fishy villain, nicknamed Bruce on-set,
continued to malfunction, forcing Steven Spielberg

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.320
and his team to figure out how to film most
of the movie without the shark.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.380
And as it turned out, the shark-free approach
worked, and Spielberg later admitted that

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.189
"had the shark been working, perhaps the film
would have made half the money and been half

00:01:39.189 --> 00:01:39.880
as scary."

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:41.540
Production woes

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.800
Between mechanical sharks sinking to the bottom
of the ocean and boats flooding mid-scene,

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.620
Jaws' seemingly endless production woes ate
into the film's production schedule and budget,

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.770
to the point where some members of the cast,
crew and executive team thought the movie

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.020
would ruin their careers.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.060
"The studio was running out of patience there's
no question about it.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.380
Things were, you know, the atmosphere was
getting a little tense."

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:11.000
According to BuzzFeed, Jaws started with budget
of $3.5 million and a 55 day production schedule,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.100
but ultimately cost about $9 million and took
over 159 days to complete.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.610
To this day, the production haunts Spielberg,
who said: "100 days over schedule, all the

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:22.660
water, it was insane.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.200
I almost got fired probably 19 times.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.060
It was almost my epilogue, you know?

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.120
'He made Jaws.'"

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.620
Of course, Jaws became the highest grossing
movie of 1975, and remains one of the most

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.140
financially successful movies of all time.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.820
But nobody knew that then.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:38.980
Actor feud

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.420
Much like the characters they played, actors
Robert Shaw and Richard Dreyfuss did not get

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.640
along during much of the shooting of Jaws.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:46.820
What caused the feud?

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.890
According to co-star Roy Scheider, Shaw initially
took issue with Dreyfuss' pompous attitude

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:53.180
about filming a movie.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.340
Scheider recalled in the Biography Channel
documentary, Jaws: The Inside Story.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.040
"[Shaw] really thought Dreyfuss needed a slapping
down, [that he was a] young punk with no stage

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:02.880
experience."

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.660
Things only got worse from there.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.640
Eventually, their feud reached a boiling point
when Dreyfuss threw a glass of bourbon that

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.560
Shaw had been drinking out the window.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.560
Spielberg remembers that moment well.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.660
"That was the shot heard around the world.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.660
The duo eventually made amends during the
filming of Quint's famous USS Indianapolis

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.090
speech — again, much like their characters.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.280
And speaking of that scene …

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:24.800
Drunk on set

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:29.269
One of the key turning points in Jaws comes
when Shaw's character Quint delivers a harrowing

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:34.409
monologue about the time he battled tiger
sharks in the water after the USS Indianapolis

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:36.200
was torpedoed by the Japanese Navy.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.819
"I don't know how many sharks maybe a thousand.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:40.950
I don't know how many men, they averaged six
an hour."

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.450
But actually filming the iconic moment wasn't
exactly easy — because Shaw was too drunk

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.380
to say his lines.

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:48.120
Spielberg told Ain't It Cool News:

00:03:48.129 --> 00:03:53.700
"I guess he had more than a few drinks because
two crew members actually had to carry him

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.409
onto the Orca and help him into his chair.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:03.680
I had two cameras on the scene and we never
got through the scene, he was just too far

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:04.680
gone."

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:09.900
Iconic ad-lib

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.440
Although Quint's speech remains one of the
great movie monologues of all times, it inevitably

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.190
pales in comparison to Jaws' most iconic line:

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.640
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.980
According to screenwriter Carl Gottlieb, the
line was a running joke on set because everybody

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:28.260
thought the barge carrying the production's
lighting, camera equipment and craft services

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.080
was too small.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.800
"[Richard] Zanuck and [David] Brown were very
stingy producers, so everyone kept telling

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.360
them, 'You're gonna need a bigger boat.'

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.220
It became a catchphrase for anytime anything
went wrong—if lunch was late or the swells

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:44.030
were rocking the camera, someone would say,
'You're gonna need a bigger boat.'"

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:47.720
Gottlieb added that Scheider liked to ad-lib
the line at various points during production

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.660
— including the now-famous scene.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.840
No sequel for Spielberg

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.700
After Jaws went on to make a boatload of money,
the inevitable sequel talk came into play.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.820
By that point, however, Spielberg was done
with the franchise.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.410
He told Ain't It Cool News: "I was done, I
was done with the ocean.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:07.460
I would have done the sequel if I hadn't had
such a horrible time at sea on the first film."

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.800
Still, that doesn't mean he hasn't revisited
the story on occasion.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.460
He added:

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:15.120
"I have a very, very good scene which I thought
would have been good for a sequel someday

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.440
… every time I think of this scene, I think,
'Hmmm, could this be another Jaws movie?'

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.160
And I have to immediately pull myself back
down to earth."

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:23.560
Oscar snub

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.580
When awards season rolled around after Jaws'
release, Spielberg was so convinced that he

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:32.240
was going to be nominated for an Oscar that
he hired a camera crew to film him watching

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.630
the nominations on television.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:40.380
"And Jaws is about to be nominated in 11 categories
you're about to see a sweep in the nominations."

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.120
Turns out Spielberg was a little too optimistic.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.680
The film wound up receiving only four nominations,
and although it went on to win three, Spielberg

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:53.400
was upset that he wasn't nominated for Best
Director for Jaws, calling the decision "commercial

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:53.900
backlash."

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.400
"When a film makes a lot of money people resent
it.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.580
Everybody… everybody loves a winner."

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.120
That may be true — but nobody loves a sore
loser, either.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.460
Thanks for watching!

